00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.000
We had made it to France.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:16.000
The last few weeks had changed us, we were stronger, fitter, more adventurous in our flight.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Our flock was now moving in perfect synchrony, whirling in the sky and performing elaborate acrobatics.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:32.000
We particularly enjoyed following coastlines where we could catch the best winds to glide over the long beaches of Brittany.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:39.000
It had been a beautiful day. The sun was setting but our leaders decided to keep flying a little longer.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:52.000
The evening air was clear and balmy. Darkness gradually fell upon us, and one by one, stars lit up the sky.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:02.000
We were flying along majestic granite cliffs, illuminated by the moonlight. We could see our shadow cast on the white rocks.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:09.000
I looked at the sky, and, suddenly Nunyati’s words came back to me.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:23.000
“Never trust the moon, Mawusi. She’s a traitor. She’ll make you believe she’s your friend by lighting up your path, but a full moon will turn you into prey.”

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:33.000
I looked back, searching for Banjoko, and that’s when I noticed three large shadows flying behind ours.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:43.000
Our leaders noticed them too. Adrenaline awoke the flock and all eighty birds sped as one body.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:51.000
“Mawusi!” I heard my friend calling me. He never called me by my name; that’s how I knew he was scared.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.000
I heard the falcons shriek too. They were close.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:01.000
I panicked. I knew too well we couldn’t outdistance them.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:07.000
What chance did we have, a group of tiny sand martins against three trained hunters?

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Our only hope was to compete against each other. I saw my friends realised it too.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:24.000
In a split second, the graceful flock was gone: all that was left was a crowd of shrieking creatures fleeing helplessly in all directions.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:33.000
I decided to dive towards the ocean, hoping the falcons wouldn’t follow me.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.000
They didn’t.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.000
I found a small cavity at the base of the cliff, almost at sea level. I hid there.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.000
My heart was racing, the wind was howling and the waves were crashing around me.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.000
I could hear the helpless cries of my friends in the distance.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:58.000
I stayed there until morning, shaking in the darkness, petrified.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.000
At sunrise, I finally dared to emerge from my shelter.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:09.000
I called for Banjoko, but all I could hear was my own voice echoed against the cliffs.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.000
The falcon had gone, and I was alone.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.000
I was completely alone.
